From: SM-X710 postmarketOS port <gts9wifi@example.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 23:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] genirq/kexec: don't take a slow-bus chip's bus lock on crash

machine_kexec_mask_interrupts() calls irq_set_irqchip_state() on every
started interrupt to clear a VM-forwarded active state.  That call takes the
chip's bus lock.  For a regmap-irq chip behind I2C, the unlock half
(regmap_irq_sync_unlock) writes the mask registers over the bus.

On the SM-X710 the four CS35L45 speaker amplifiers expose 48 such interrupts.
In a crash kexec the I2C write runtime-resumes the GENI controller, which
votes interconnect bandwidth through RPMh and waits for an RPMh completion
interrupt.  That interrupt never arrives with the other CPUs stopped and
interrupts disabled, so every write times out after 10 s and the panicking
kernel never reaches the crash kernel.

A chip that needs a bus lock is never one that forwards interrupts to a VM,
so skip the call for it.  irq_shutdown() still runs and only updates the
chip's cached mask, which is what the bus lock would have flushed.

Signed-off-by: SM-X710 postmarketOS port <gts9wifi@example.com>
---
--- a/kernel/irq/kexec.c
+++ b/kernel/irq/kexec.c
@@ -20,7 +20,15 @@
 		if (!chip || !irqd_is_started(&desc->irq_data))
 			continue;
 
-		if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_GENERIC_IRQ_KEXEC_CLEAR_VM_FORWARD)) {
+		/*
+		 * irq_set_irqchip_state() takes the chip's bus lock.  On a chip
+		 * behind a slow bus (regmap-irq over I2C/SPI) that is a mutex
+		 * and a bus transfer, which cannot complete here: the other CPUs
+		 * are stopped and interrupts are off, so a crash kexec hangs.
+		 * Such a chip is never forwarded to a VM, so skip the call.
+		 */
+		if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_GENERIC_IRQ_KEXEC_CLEAR_VM_FORWARD) &&
+		    !chip->irq_bus_lock && !chip->irq_bus_sync_unlock) {
 			/*
 			 * First try to remove the active state from an interrupt which is forwarded
 			 * to a VM. If the interrupt is not forwarded, try to EOI the interrupt.
